 ## Unreleased
 
+- speech: a voice file can hold several reference passages, spoken into the prompt in order. `drama_voice.py combine A.json B.json C.json --out NAME.json --source-note ...` writes `drama3-voice/2`: one `source`, and a `references` list whose entries keep each passage's text, codes (byte for byte), digests and clip `audio_sha256`. Naming such a voice builds the same prompt rows as a request sending the first passages as `references` for a voice holding the last. Single-reference `drama3-voice/1` files load and build their prompts as before; `/2` is a new format so a server that reads only `/1` refuses it rather than speak from one passage. A voice whose passages leave no prompt row for an input stops the server at startup (`voice references too long`). `GET /v1/voices` adds each voice's `references` count, and its `frames` and `seconds` count all of them ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md#several-references-in-one-voice))
 - speech: `drama_voice_clip.py` tells two speakers apart by timbre instead of pitch, so an expressive single voice is no longer refused for its pitch range. A weighted two-means split puts its stretches of speech in two groups by their mel cepstra (6 ms frames to 16 kHz), and a split `sqrt(T² / rank)` over 5.6 is refused, T² being the groups' two-sample Hotelling statistic over the rank whitened directions; groups apart in a direction neither varies in are refused outright. The threshold is empirical, set on model-invented voices and synthetic mixes of them only: one voice measured up to 4.87 and two in a full-band take from 6.40, margins of 0.73 and 0.80 on a small set. A take with fewer than two stretches of speech, or a spectrum too degenerate to measure, is refused instead of passed. The report adds `speaker_split`; `pitch_spread_semitones` refuses nothing now ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md))
 - speech: a voice file says whose voice it holds. `source` is `{kind: model-invented | owner | consented, note}`, the operator's attestation, and `--voices` refuses a file without one or with a blank note. `GET /v1/voices` lists the loaded voices (name, frames, seconds, source kind); there is no upload route. `tools/zig/drama_voice_clip.py` prepares a take: mono 44.1 kHz aimed at -20 dBFS speech level within -1 dBFS peaks, cut at a pause to a 10-30 s clip, refused when short, clipped, noisy (judged on the kept clip), over music or hum, or voiced by more than one speaker. Its transcript is either the take's, kept only when the cut leaves no loud frame out, or a local Whisper draft to correct; a confirmed transcript is never paired with a re-prepared clip. `drama_voice.py` encodes with `--reference` or `--native` when given, else with a `tensorfold-native` on `PATH` whose `--help` lists `voice encode` ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md))
 - speech: `tensorfold-native voice encode CLIP.wav --model DIR --text T --name NAME --out NAME.json` writes a Drama 3 voice file with a native CUDA codec encoder (DAC encoder, pre_module and RVQ in BF16), no Python; on three of our test clips, 2.5 to 3 % of its semantic codes differ from the reference's ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md))
